m.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y)=4.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $4.$ The function reaches this value at $(x,y)=(-3,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/39b694d4ea57e59d97102963009322aeef84d5fa58df07dbc854a73972ad7458.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163838/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ee9cbe8-ad40-5155-b42d-1802f36adf27"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163838"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection/262efaee666a88aace8a75783856ad0ced1ec64c45a854127f92d18b1f921056" :answer/id #uuid "83fb5253-304a-5b7a-ab5e-93a07455ace2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection/4b7249b279275187b39c38bb448e56c0526d0fb949a1a6f36d04fa708923fc3e" :answer/id #uuid "354a71fa-f5bc-5ad1-b9e8-a398c041b4c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $D$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "93058205-6480-5aa2-85e4-c30d41bb9faa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" :answer/id #uuid "3163c71d-e169-5a64-9209-bea7bf4b1261" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2" :answer/id #uuid "0fc74907-b0fd-5835-a7bf-99d7eaa70d58" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e64a371-2dd8-5f56-86f9-2722bb59fa57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-164164/selection/4b7249b279275187b39c38bb448e56c0526d0fb949a1a6f36d04fa708923fc3e" :answer-field/choices ["q-164164/selection/262efaee666a88aace8a75783856ad0ced1ec64c45a854127f92d18b1f921056" "q-164164/selection/4b7249b279275187b39c38bb448e56c0526d0fb949a1a6f36d04fa708923fc3e" "q-164164/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-164164/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" "q-164164/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-164164" :question/id #uuid "4e3accfb-30b6-5bbc-a8c8-456e487631e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164164" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/9bafb9e41a34d03b70ca2b06bbb8c498f12b51f6e1625ddcd3a95fc4edbbccb6.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $D.$ Hence, $B$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D({x}_{D},{y}_{D},{z}_{D}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{D},{y}_{D})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariab