 on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $B$ and $C.$ Hence, $B$ and $C$ are local maximum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $B({x}_{B},{y}_{B},{z}_{B}),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f({x}_{B},{y}_{B})=f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y)\\le f(a,b)$, the point $B$ does satisfy the conditions for a local maximum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6d98be34c848e0fbce571fc572fe9dae244e6ce80b677063fea2e19983e1a2da.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-164162/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-164162"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer/id #uuid "4d417d15-4f51-5d2a-9b0f-291a824d2a86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" :answer/id #uuid "3741b59c-a4fa-5bee-93b8-cb654017b94c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570" :answer/id #uuid "493a6275-b46f-5a2e-8f48-a08cc49243f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" :answer/id #uuid "b47b486f-8644-5c82-ba9c-d580ba14bfb7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" :answer/id #uuid "ea55dade-6216-525a-946e-3a6289707806" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "67d5c1f7-7089-5393-8a46-7a04e9f0ea9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163784/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-163784/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" "q-163784/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" "q-163784/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570" "q-163784/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" "q-163784/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163784" :question/id #uuid "59c2ea9a-a8ee-5204-9030-a96b5132c460" :question/math-academy-id "q-163784" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈6+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-2-\\sqrt{2}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-2+\\sqrt{2}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163784/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163784"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-163787"] :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-3+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,{\\lambda }_{2}=-\\sqrt{6}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=\\sqrt{6},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that ${f}^{″}(a)$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-2<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-\\sqrt{6}<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =\\sqrt{6}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite. Therefore, $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" :answer/id #uuid "4ab45379-3c25-5e55-8ce8-b01155026eda" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" :answer/id #uuid "d16af734-0eaf-57bd-8bf3-6e05ac780b32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" :answer/id #uuid "38db8491-eba7-5938-adb8-76998d967c83" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer/id #uuid "54202e6a-ca21-5ed2-9e40-cf632b44eaa5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570" :answer/id #uuid "55b1c31b-3817-5df4-8237-86921347da6b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "444bab0f-2b2d-5995-8132-8c111b8d4dc5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163782/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-163782/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" "q-163782/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" "q-163782/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" "q-163782/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" "q-163782/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163782" :question/id #uuid "2a1216b6-1e9a-5f9f-bce6-998f55ac7a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-163782" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-12 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -12\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,{\\lambda }_{2}=-16$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-16,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-4<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-16<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =-16<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163782/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163782"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer/id #uuid "66a08d21-9e20-5dc7-8693-9747f47f25a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" :answer/id #uuid "a00a35ad-2cfa-53d5-9172-fe587106dddc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" :answer/id #uuid "a08b0a06-fe6a-50b6-932b-b6727dad6ff8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" :answer/id #uuid "be6765cf-4c15-5738-b06d-d353e2139e8b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570" :answer/id #uuid "07fd156b-7df4-512b-8063-bc909728c83e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e95208bd-7c1d-5d62-a714-08aa876e0e4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163785/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" :answer-field/choices ["q-163785/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" "q-163785/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" "q-163785/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" "q-163785/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" "q-163785/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163785" :question/id #uuid "0b8937f2-04e6-5039-91a2-37342d44c09a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163785" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,{\\lambda }_{2}=3-\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3+\\sqrt{2},$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that all the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =8>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =3-\\sqrt{2}>0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{3} & =3+\\sqrt{2}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is positive definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163785/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163785"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" :answer/id #uuid "f6a56826-c42b-504f-8467-8dc1e2ab4d4b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "saddle point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer/id #uuid "484fba29-ad87-5941-8a53-43f7352534bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local maximum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" :answer/id #uuid "8ae40e71-9967-57fa-97ce-0ea0e77be200" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "local minimum" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" :answer/id #uuid "771004f8-8857-5781-841e-3fe26ffad10c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "not a critical point" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570" :answer/id #uuid "4d58a639-4330-588f-a6e1-70732ae91942" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "inconclusive" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "740517b9-c4d7-5c42-a662-a02676b9507d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163773/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" :answer-field/choices ["q-163773/selection/6de4efb3da69cba6bde7c08a860d050f0989ef93796e738e2cf90f562d80dd16" "q-163773/selection/1de08c6a44b911276a6486b71fa0f68b0e3e339fec4be3acef35c3b8382044b4" "q-163773/selection/61221198a142a5fd79ec443fe1fa196393d6513af9f32fba6c5a3fcc16b7da76" "q-163773/selection/48a24429df7ebafa7405ba6572b1eafd382515abe248e50f2b1b24400fa2ad8b" "q-163773/selection/2103cde231067b090ccdabfdd43abfd9cc996c6bde2c0140b489b115e9e86570"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163773" :question/id #uuid "d168a2de-e2b9-56ef-8474-c9a37568dd8a" :question/math-academy-id "q-163773" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{aligned}](x-a).\n$$\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ what type of point is $a?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{n}$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(x-a)$ term, we can conclude that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ Hence, $a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of ${f}^{″}(a)$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-163773/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "233eab4c-ab17-53a8-ae03-f6f28ac78922"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163773"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-163723"] :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-9+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=6$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=6$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=6>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}|=20>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=40>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d46b0f80-a0a2-531c-95bc-09ddcf42e64b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "15f593dc-24e3-587f-a32d-15ad7e54cbb4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "817c24eb-deda-5799-a106-809cee182e82" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "3e46d9ba-8e56-58c7-87fb-07269209b82d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "183e3535-691b-570e-a2e3-fe769a056a28" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c52db7f7-1984-5961-9739-668e55ca9b34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163780/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-163780/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-163780/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-163780/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-163780/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-163780/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163780" :question/id #uuid "bf80131f-4c10-5348-8930-14c545cdb7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-163780" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=-1$ and $det(A)=4$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-1,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-1<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}|=2>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163780/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163780"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "1805b973-5197-55f4-8b8f-19ea2e135f38" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "8b0caf17-0145-58c4-be74-1e643be463ea" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "efc5ef9b-2b40-501e-95b2-16c1db904823" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "715bea73-2a15-5f9c-bd75-3dada3f4a170" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "14a197c9-f7f2-5b26-b427-5576df080cd2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2cf4ee5-e508-5fa4-a161-1128ff63e5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163779/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-163779/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-163779/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-163779/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-163779/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-163779/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163779" :question/id #uuid "82f753ea-7964-53a3-b90b-5bef2adf907f" :question/math-academy-id "q-163779" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈4+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=5$ and $det(A)=2$\n- $A$ is positive-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, let's recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=5,$ but\n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=5>0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|=1>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is positive-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is positive-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local minimum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163779/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163779"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "03000afe-754d-5dd1-92cb-b6b477f8f29d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "a2b05b0f-edb0-5e7c-924d-24fd8862b141" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "4223607c-12a8-5a81-8a61-c56245afd59a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "d2aafe14-e796-500d-af03-5bd70a908eff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "ac071e6f-3543-5cb8-a153-e1b55ac5c827" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c351ded6-159d-5c1d-9ebb-de5d1b4c9635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-163791/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-163791/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-163791/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-163791/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-163791/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163791" :question/id #uuid "dd7fe5a7-b487-5ac8-904d-7b5106eafa05" :question/math-academy-id "q-163791" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈8+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $det(A)=-19$\n- $A$ is negative-definite\n- $a$ is a local minimum of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2(11)-1(-3) \\\\  & =-19.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-2<0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}|=7>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-19<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the matrix $A$ is negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a local maximum of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-163791/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163791"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" :answer/id #uuid "c8e79d92-1e4f-5356-8edc-f419bcbd23a8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f5f3a826-8f7c-5d25-8583-ffe374b193f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" :answer/id #uuid "38e0d79b-98eb-5c55-b05f-196979dd7c1c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "3db35873-1853-5460-a158-f79ca4c3fa5b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9f2a2949-7385-5c27-83af-84c0af6f2864" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "485600a2-9349-5a7b-bd6a-6faca42ba3c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-163789/selection/03b99debc8e9534f2364927a2cf1eba83bddd906503dea5d13395ce906a45ab9" "q-163789/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-163789/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-163789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-163789/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163789" :question/id #uuid "a28cb23f-b8e0-5435-a525-0125a81f2be0" :question/math-academy-id "q-163789" :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(x)$ about $x=a\\in {R}^{3}$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈16+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}A(x-a),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${a}_{11}=3$ and $det(A)>0$\n- $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- $a$ is a saddle point of $f$" :question/worked-solution "The point $a\\in {R}^{n}$ is a critical point of the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}.$ In such cases, we can classify $a$ as follows:\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is positive definite, then $a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is negative definite, then $a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if ${f}^{″}(a)$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A={f}^{″}(a).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=3,$ but\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3(3)-1(-2)-4(7) \\\\  & =-17<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=3>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=5>0 \\\\ {Δ}_{3} & =|A|=-17<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-163789/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "40d58ee6-1121-5ddc-a96f-1a7fa7a193dd"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163789"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12460"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b550d203e3be327eb2af27a1035b6693953dd2802bb203af06d5773e48d609c3.png)\n\nThe graph $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. What are the global maximum points of the function?" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following definitions:\n\n- The global maximum of a function is the largest value it attains on its domain. There can only be (at most) one global maximum.\n\n- A global maximum point is a point $(x,y,z)$ where a function reaches a global maximum. There can be zero, one, or many global maximum points.\n\nFor our function, the maximum value reached by the function is $f(x,y) = 2.$ Therefore, the global maximum is $2.$ The function reaches this value when $(x,y) = (0,-2),$ so the global maximum point is $(0,-2,2).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f80d19126252f6ab7097cf528aec9726c2708f48c815c026f632fccd77cf74d4.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12461"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/6c6906429cf946f196b959cce7c20589b6212ae45df9607e15c6385be9aef858.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $z=f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are local minimum points?" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nSuch regions exist for the points $A, B,$ and $D.$ Hence, $A, B,$ and $D$ are local minimum points.\n\n**Note**: For the point $D(x_D,y_D,z_D),$ there are points $(x,y)$ in the surrounding regions such that $f(x_D,y_D) = f(x,y).$ However, since the definition of local extrema is non-strict, $f(x,y) \\geq f(a,b)$, the point $D$ does satisfy the conditions for a local minimum.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/cdb83ac891799788c8289f7e44902bf6be008cb13a7bf27f330b48113df021d1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12465"] :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function $f(\\mathbf x)$ about $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a\\in\\mathbb R^2$ is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2ae5b42ae953d1b9d3fd642e9938924a9b2bc439dee1ea52c868f186798b1520.png)\n\nGiven that the eigenvalues of $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are $\\lambda_1 = -1$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3,$ what type of point is $\\mathbf{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf{a}$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf{a}$ is a saddle point\n\nThe quadratic Taylor expansion of a function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ about $\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf{x}) \\approx f(\\mathbf{a}) + \\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a})(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})\n+\n\\dfrac12(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})^T\n\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})\n(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a}).\n$$\n\nSince the quadratic Taylor expansion of $f$ has no $(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{a})$ term, we can conclude that $\\boldsymbol{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ Hence, $\\mathbf a$ is a critical point of $f.$\n\nFinally, notice that both the eigenvalues of $\\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & =-1<0 \\\\ {\\lambda }_{2} & =-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix is negative definite. Therefore, $\\mathbf{a}$ is a local maximum of $f.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7030"] :question/problem "The quadratic Taylor approximation of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e21f1a654e7ec74a637369e2330d0e0f3ccd0a3469bc15405b9ae581aaa4efba.png) about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/e2a3ebddc9b6755b15eca1e5497dfc859ea5c2d6bfb5ee0a98df1c6476f87a4f.png) is given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a21655b6405345e52c0f18c50e098409ca06e2b938ee77dc1b839c54f5f60456.png)\n\nwhere \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a963b613e053f027bc0434bacaba0056c70493b72181eff33f97cd16d39afc37.png) \n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8317d25a4b5a486cf86db32d0868ca8135c4701abf2e2f579b59ce7f4c3ec05b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/5db28cc61ee0aadd0ccc482cacf12c3a851ff803af7927d3bb918bc9c7c72ffc.png)\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8f3cbdf2ea946537b9792aba2720fb22db787b8b72be93a3c0a5308ff111ee13.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite\n- ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/42f776502d5c552cefc008f7ea701f0f45578326e649b3f4dcd4bc548738d0ef.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fb7b36c5e6bd7e8c334d8874eb7a781085930a078aadd1cabc79475006fa894c.png)" :question/worked-solution "The point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7ac5ab8e5444901eb7e978a0916e953bbe6da7ff8fb8a481537ad1f07d5bf48d.png) is a critical point of the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/87f22c24143e0fdad241f6b070b0e0ba1d89b2d5c32669989a7c75df700f5e75.png) if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/2fa6429606f96f4c4f43b91de76acab79152fd9989108836084cea77e0d9db09.png) In such cases, we can classify ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1287cfe4aa57a8ec4c01f84934cb29b3d524f0524a48e39d7f6a30aed97e8ee2.png) as follows:\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is positive definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a local minimum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/91ecc7d66921c91ed5baf1b1d2453f9cc4678feed21f325291b38f103f0266b6.png) is negative definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/74bbb71a684ae2b42d8a3779704a0c8b47ef43e5cebd265861418f77cd2b6267.png) is a local maximum\n\n- if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/f267e59d3059eac794b9f97c216e0dafd1b01c00ea5a0466d01cdab5fdeef865.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/81a814457abc7dc83baedc3d0ccd3be23e5734c3634a0bc8c8ce7fca53cc9406.png) is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1ada0c06e92bc2e76f33fade0b4f28728f641cc98923f855735c118d2ea0ba25.png) Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/29aa6115eeac65ed8ca2194e8fdce4de48591b144de9ee298e7c151f78bda4ac.png) for ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ffb48b96eb46bc28ce8546552edbc23496ae84720dc2cc4413cc7425b056bba0.png)  denote the leading principal minors of an ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/363026acf3cf01ffee6edd4166c849b1cce0cfd1e14af8dc3a15fa2a2f41163d.png) matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/50946bb3afa72276eb132efe89bbe450df8836747fa6d11d04dbb142da7f6fad.png) Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/407d3bd58712c649860e47e39322f4471da8446ed312741900b2697fafe9d4a5.png) (as well as the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/849d81b747384fa1c4d5194327dd049207fdb7bf2db258e117c7214f870555b3.png)) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/a93d47764597bd39da3f5bb625a6d1977c61f6a731e8b041850fef5dc9c7bc52.png) for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/8161c7c2b38d84c7033c688b3b41d0decc2861cd218f08dc3f87625eed2cc376.png)\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/127d0aace5f471d897e0b6171e68da877234f9b709bf379245032522de9f6aa5.png) we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/b18d3603f870bb527a4ee4618ed482165e62659a001cf8f2bbef4503c249b313.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/541f4f6d68d831274235815dceef666c2a59172ab8825d63aa189957530d9ce9.png) is odd, and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/32a94e6ea1436d78a428c8ff22e50a7df311609ab74824c672abd2334c83988a.png) when ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/aebdc0a3ecd72c42a452c68c6019eff7ad41de49b8a09517f7fd2ff8df58e32f.png) is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/fcb57eaada70434abbf4a6056c9a8a793f6a0348196813e6a512de4772290f8e.png) but\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/ae6614c135f2e9d1d011de5f58d17fb4e43c2c6b87a7831a57b1287cf1a6e13d.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/1851f51854993b0360e75b0fc717263a7a84938b86ae515d74be32a0ac87362d.png)\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/7906e975aaeffa8eae6d319b4be2d6a585c41b95ec59d721eedb7355fda29066.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/3397629141c3034fbd4b5cd5bd8c554676175a4b1e3f5eccd26cc5d66ee1bec8.png) is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/27f36b1d1846e12e3c50b7d4e47f5745727792d52a48e4d85b689ec36e3d25a0.png) is a saddle point of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070363/assets/4f118f558380017fb67aa8f0b030dfa06a10216b7381582c619907c32988f211.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
